%erg 0.1
Title: Text-less scanned PDFs silently count as indexed
Created: 2026-09-09
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-09-09T13:25Z MinhHaDuong created

--- body ---
## Context

Found while derisking ticket 0744's "No text layer" Open item (was the
class visible as `failed-session`, or hidden inside silent `current`?). It
is the second: `inspect()`'s pack-reading branch (bootstrap.js:1977-1988)
only distinguishes `current` from `unsupported-pack`/`stale-source`/
`stale-processor`/`invalid-pack` by comparing versions, the source hash and
the processor identity. Nothing there reads how much text the pack actually
carries. A scanned PDF with no text layer produces a well-formed pack —
correct magic header, parses cleanly, every identity field matches — with
near-zero extracted content, and it reads as `current`: successfully
indexed.

This is not hypothetical. Measured on `~/data/Zotero-fresh/zotero.sqlite`
(Zotero not running, queried read-only): of 1,195 existing
`.zotero-sdt-cache` packs sampled, 61 (5.1%) carry under 20 characters of
extracted text across their PDF's first 5 pages despite the pack existing —
spot-checked 5 of those pack files directly, all well-formed (correct
`\x89SDT\r\n\x1a\n` header, 462-767 bytes, consistent with near-empty
content, not a stub or a truncated write). Scaled to that library's ~9,263
PDF attachments, this is a population in the low hundreds — large enough to
move the "Files indexed X/Y" figure users already see today, not a rounding
error.

Zotero's own `fulltextItems.totalChars`/`indexedChars` were checked first as
a cheaper proxy and found NULL for all 9,197 PDF rows in this library/
version — dead in this environment, not usable as a shortcut here.

This is upstream of and independent of ticket 0744: whether "current" means
"successfully indexed" is a correctness question that matters regardless of
whether 0744's grouping UI ever ships, and 0744's own "No text layer"
subsection needs whatever status this ticket introduces to exist first.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — `inspect()`'s pack-reading branch,
  `~1963-1988`, where `current` is currently decided
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` — `SDT_STATUS_CLASSES`, where any new
  status needs a home (not `indexed` — that arithmetic must stop counting an
  empty pack as coverage)
- Ticket 0744 — `bootstrap.js`'s "Unindexed attachments" design;
  its "No text layer" class row is currently sourced to `failed-session` by
  supposition, corrected by this ticket's finding

## Actions

1. Investigate whether the pack's own metadata (`reader.getMetadata()`, or
   a field in the pack body itself) exposes a character/page-content count
   cheaply. The measurement above used an external `pdftotext` pass as a
   verification tool, not a reading of the pack's own declared content —
   find out whether the native SDT pack format already carries something
   this cheap to check on every census, before assuming the fix needs
   re-parsing text on every sweep.
2. Add a status distinguishing a well-formed-but-empty pack from a genuinely
   current one; classify it out of `indexed`, so coverage arithmetic stops
   counting it as success.
3. Update `getSDTCoverage` and the census account so the corrected
   classification is reflected everywhere the old count was read.
4. Cross-reference from ticket 0744, whose "No text layer" subsection reads
   this status once it exists.

## Test

- Red first: a fixture pack with well-formed headers/identity but near-zero
  extracted content currently reads `current`; must read the new status
  after the fix.
- Red first: a genuinely full-text pack is unaffected — the new check must
  not misclassify real content as empty.

## Verification

- [ ] The new status exists, is classified outside `indexed`, and is named
      clearly (not `unsupported` or `invalid-pack`, which already mean
      something else)
- [ ] `getSDTCoverage`'s totals reflect the corrected classification
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Invariants

- No change to what the sitter reads or writes on disk; this corrects a
  classification, not the extraction itself.
- The census account's total still equals the rows above it.

## Exit criteria

A well-formed pack carrying no meaningful extracted text is classified
distinctly from a genuinely current one, and "Files indexed X/Y" no longer
counts it as coverage.
